>> title: Shōgayaki
>> category: Stir-Fries
>> tags: japanese, pork, ginger, weeknight, quick, pan-fried
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: 生姜焼き, shōgayaki, shogayaki, しょうがやき, 豚の生姜焼き, buta no shogayaki, ginger pork, pork ginger
>> pairs-with: gohan, miso-soup
>> servings: 2
>> time: 20 min
>> slack: narrow — the loin is a quarter inch thick and goes from juicy to grey in well under a minute a side; the tare can always be reduced further, and the pork cannot be un-cooked
>> step.1: mix one part each
>> step.2: dust and sear 1 min a side
>> step.3: pour in, reduce 2 min
>> step.4: spoon onto the cabbage

Stir @grated ginger{2%Tbs}(30 mL; from a thumb of ginger, grated skin and all) into @soy sauce{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @sake{2%Tbs}(30 mL) and @mirin{2%Tbs}(30 mL) in a #bowl{}. One part each of the four, and that is the entire seasoning — enough for about 400 g of thin-sliced pork.

Dust @pork loin{14%oz}(400 g; in 1/4-inch slices, the rim of fat nicked every inch so it does not curl) with @plain flour{1%Tbs} and sear it in @neutral oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) in a #frying pan{} ~sear{2%min}, one layer, about a minute a side. The flour is what makes the tare cling instead of running off.

Add @onion{1/2}(sliced thin) to @&(~1)pork{}, pour @&(1)ginger tare{} down the side of the pan, and ~reduce{2%min}, turning the slices through it until the sauce goes syrupy and coats.

Pile @&(~1)shōgayaki{} onto @white cabbage{3%cups}(shredded fine, held in ice water 10 min and drained dry). The cabbage is not garnish: it is cold and raw and it is what the sauce is eaten with.
